systemd-cryptenroll supports enrolling TPM2, as well as PKCS#11 or FIDO2
tokens. You can also keep relying on the password you set earlier. Unless
cryptographically secure, using a password that is not hardware-protected
provides weaker protection compared to a hardware token.

It is recommended to have at least two secrets enrolled, one for everyday use
and one for recovery, in case the primary key gets lost. Neither secret should
be a regular password. If you do not have access to two hardware tokens,
systemd-cryptenroll also provides the option to enroll "recovery keys". These
technically work the same as user-defined passwords but are cryptographically
secure as well as non-memorable but easy to input. They are not designed for
regular use, instead you should store them at a secure location and make use of
them if you lose access to your primary key.

Enter "none" once you have finished enrolling your tokens. Afterwards, you will
be given the option to wipe your user password from the LUKS header and/or
enroll recovery keys.
